WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.845
Lexical containment reasons about path
text.

2
00:00:02.925 --> 00:00:06.053
The filesystem may resolve that text
through symbolic links,

3
00:00:06.133 --> 00:00:08.779
Windows junctions or other reparse points,
mounts,

4
00:00:08.859 --> 00:00:10.783
and platform-specific indirection.

5
00:00:10.863 --> 00:00:15.133
A candidate can appear beneath the
configured root while an intermediate directory

6
00:00:15.213 --> 00:00:17.559
link directs the operating system
elsewhere.

7
00:00:17.639 --> 00:00:21.668
path.resolve and path.relative cannot
detect this because they do not inspect

8
00:00:21.748 --> 00:00:22.831
filesystem objects.

9
00:00:22.911 --> 00:00:26.700
The security decision may need to account
for the object actually reached.

10
00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:27.982
For an existing target,

11
00:00:28.062 --> 00:00:32.393
fs.realpath resolves parent components and
symbolic links to a canonical pathname.

12
00:00:32.473 --> 00:00:35.780
An application can canonicalize the
trusted root and candidate,

13
00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:39.529
then apply the same path.relative
containment rule to those real paths.

14
00:00:39.609 --> 00:00:42.676
Both paths must be interpreted in the same
platform context.

15
00:00:42.756 --> 00:00:46.906
This is appropriate when the threat model
permits links inside or near storage and

16
00:00:46.986 --> 00:00:49.091
the operation acts on an existing object.

17
00:00:49.171 --> 00:00:51.997
A lexical check remains useful before
filesystem work,

18
00:00:52.077 --> 00:00:54.783
but the canonical check addresses a
different layer.

19
00:00:54.863 --> 00:00:56.668
Realpath is not a universal answer.

20
00:00:56.748 --> 00:01:00.597
A target for creation does not yet exist,
so its real path cannot be obtained.

21
00:01:00.677 --> 00:01:03.503
The application can canonicalize the
existing parent,

22
00:01:03.583 --> 00:01:07.853
verify that parent against the real root,
and choose a server-generated child name.

23
00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:10.820
That still depends on controlling the
parent directory.

24
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:13.426
Real filesystem state can change after the
check,

25
00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:17.355
and hard links or mounted layouts may not
match a simplistic pathname model.

26
00:01:17.435 --> 00:01:21.705
Node also does not expose identical
race-resistant primitives for every operation

27
00:01:21.785 --> 00:01:22.506
and platform.

28
00:01:22.586 --> 00:01:26.917
Design storage so untrusted parties cannot
create links or rename directories along

29
00:01:26.997 --> 00:01:28.440
security-sensitive paths.

30
00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.444
Protect root and parent permissions,

31
00:01:30.524 --> 00:01:32.870
keep temporary content in private
locations,

32
00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:37.039
and avoid mixing user-controlled directory
trees with privileged operations.

33
00:01:37.119 --> 00:01:41.329
On Windows, include junctions and reparse
points in deployment review rather than

34
00:01:41.409 --> 00:01:43.574
checking only Unix-style symbolic links.

35
00:01:43.654 --> 00:01:47.022
Canonical comparison strengthens an
existing-object workflow,

36
00:01:47.102 --> 00:01:51.372
but permissions, authorization, and
check-then-use design determine whether the

37
00:01:51.452 --> 00:01:54.820
containment decision remains valid when
the filesystem changes.
